public class Solution {
	public static int mostCostEfficientCar(Car petrolCar, Car dieselCar) {
  		// Write your code here.

		  long petrolFuel = ((long) petrolCar.numberOfKilometerCarRunInaMonth *
		   6 * petrolCar.pricePerLiter)/petrolCar.numberOfkilometerCarRunInOneLiter;

		  long petrolTotal = petrolCar.priceOfCar + (long)petrolCar.maintenanceCostPerMonth * 6 + petrolFuel;

		  long dieselFuel = ((long) dieselCar.numberOfKilometerCarRunInaMonth *
		  6 * dieselCar.pricePerLiter) / dieselCar.numberOfkilometerCarRunInOneLiter;

		  long dieselTotal = dieselCar.priceOfCar + (long)dieselCar.maintenanceCostPerMonth * 6 +dieselFuel;

		  if(petrolTotal < dieselTotal) return 0;
		  else if(dieselTotal < petrolTotal) return 1;
		  else return -1;
	}
}


You are given the price of ‘Petrol’ cars, the maintenance cost of the car per month, number of kilometers a car runs in one liter, amount of Petrol per liter, and number of kilometers a car can run per month. 
Similar details are also given for ‘Diesel’ cars.
Your task is to find which car ( ‘Petrol’ or ‘Diesel’ ) is efficient to use for an exact 6 month period. If your answer is ‘Petrol’ car then print ‘0’, if your answer is ‘Diesel’ car then print ‘1’, 
if both the cars ( ‘Petrol’ and ‘Diesel’) are efficient then print ‘-1’.
For example, consider both the cars:
Price of ‘Petrol’ car = ‘780000’, the maintenance cost of ‘Petrol’ car per month = ‘500’, number of kilometers a ‘Petrol’ car can run in one liter  = ’35’, price of ‘Petrol’ per liter = ‘75’ and number of kilometers 
a ‘Petrol’ car can run in a month = ‘145’.
Similar details are given for ‘Diesel’ car, price of ‘Diesel’ car = ‘850000’, the maintenance cost of ‘Diesel’ car per month = ‘1000’, number of kilometers a ‘Diesel’ car can run in a one-liter = ’30’, price of 
‘Diesel’ per liter = ‘80’ and number of kilometers a ‘Diesel’ car can run in a month = ‘200’.
Then the ‘Petrol’ car will be more efficient, so we will print the integer ‘0’.


T.C => O(1)
S.C => O(1)
